A reply I posted yesterday seems to have got lost in transmission. Basically, I agreed with Chris and Eric and follow that path myself. On Eric's last point: I do wonder how one can avoid models in which the same covariate is used for both detection and density, as often they will be linked (e.g. because home range size and sigma decline with increasing density). Maybe the answer is in sigma-lambda0 compensation, allowed for in the 'a0' models (health warning: these are mildly ad hoc, there are fishhooks, and they can be awkward to fit, so not for the fainthearted). To be specific, a model D~X, k~1, a0~1 should allow both sigma and lambda0 to vary with covariate X, to the extent of the compensatory relationships sigma = k/sqrt(D(X)), and lambda0 = a0/(2 pi sigma^2), where estimating k substitutes for estimating sigma. It may be necessary to specify starting values for the parameters to get the numerical likelihood maximisation to work.
Murray

